Relevant columns and their potential usage  
player.player_id: joins to every fact table to know who the player is  
player.player_name: final reporting / readability  
match.match_id: the key that joins the fact tables (ball‑by‑ball and wicket) to a season  
match.season_id: groups everything by season  
match.team_1 / match.team_2: not directly needed here  
batsman_scored.match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no / runs_scored: sum of runs for every player‑ball; inner‑join on (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no) to ball_by_ball so the striker (= batsman) is known  
ball_by_ball.striker: player_id of the batsman who faced the ball  
wicket_taken.match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no / player_out / kind_out: counts wickets for player_out (the dismissed batter) or, if you want the bowler, you still use ball_by_ball.bowler together with the wicket table; here we want “how many wickets a bowler took” so we need bowler from ball_by_ball and filter wicket kinds != 'run out' AND != 'hit wicket' AND != 'retired hurt'  
ball_by_ball.bowler: player_id of bowler; used to credit wickets  
Conditions that will be useful  
•  wicket_taken.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')  
•  batsman_scored.runs_scored IS NOT NULL (implicitly)  
•  joins always on (match_id,over_id,ball_id,innings_no) between the three granular tables  

Below are ten exploratory / building‑block queries (each limited to at most 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the final combined data set.

```sql
-- 1. Distinct season IDs available
SELECT DISTINCT season_id
FROM match
ORDER BY season_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at dismissal kinds present
SELECT DISTINCT kind_out
FROM wicket_taken
ORDER BY kind_out
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Show a small sample of striker‑to‑runs linkage (after join) for sanity
SELECT bbb.match_id,
       bbb.over_id,
       bbb.ball_id,
       bbb.striker        AS batsman_id,
       bs.runs_scored
FROM ball_by_ball AS bbb
JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
  ON  bs.match_id  = bbb.match_id
  AND bs.over_id   = bbb.over_id
  AND bs.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
  AND bs.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Total runs by every batsman in one season (e.g. 2010)
SELECT m.season_id,
       bbb.striker                     AS player_id,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored)             AS total_runs
FROM match            AS m
JOIN ball_by_ball     AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
JOIN batsman_scored   AS bs  ON bs.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                           AND bs.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                           AND bs.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                           AND bs.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
WHERE m.season_id = 3
GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.striker
ORDER BY total_runs DESC, player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Runs per player per season (all seasons, no ranking yet)
SELECT m.season_id,
       bbb.striker        AS player_id,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS total_runs
FROM match          AS m
JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
JOIN batsman_scored AS bs  ON bs.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                          AND bs.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                          AND bs.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                          AND bs.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.striker
ORDER BY m.season_id, total_runs DESC, player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Wickets credited to bowlers per season, excluding the three unwanted kinds
SELECT m.season_id,
       bbb.bowler          AS player_id,
       COUNT(*)            AS wickets
FROM match          AS m
JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
JOIN wicket_taken   AS wt  ON wt.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                          AND wt.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                          AND wt.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                          AND wt.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')
GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.bowler
ORDER BY m.season_id, wickets DESC, player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Attach player names to the wicket summary above for readability
SELECT m.season_id,
       pl.player_id,
       pl.player_name,
       COUNT(*) AS wickets
FROM match          AS m
JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
JOIN wicket_taken   AS wt  ON wt.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                          AND wt.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                          AND wt.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                          AND wt.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
JOIN player         AS pl  ON pl.player_id = bbb.bowler
WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')
GROUP BY m.season_id, pl.player_id, pl.player_name
ORDER BY m.season_id, wickets DESC, pl.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Rank batsmen per season and keep only top three (using window functions)
SELECT season_id,
       player_id,
       total_runs
FROM (
    SELECT m.season_id,
           bbb.striker AS player_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS total_runs,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY m.season_id
               ORDER BY SUM(bs.runs_scored) DESC, bbb.striker
           ) AS rn
    FROM match AS m
    JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
    JOIN batsman_scored AS bs  ON bs.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                              AND bs.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                              AND bs.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                              AND bs.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
    GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.striker
)
WHERE rn <= 3
ORDER BY season_id, rn
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Rank bowlers per season and keep only top three
SELECT season_id,
       player_id,
       wickets
FROM (
    SELECT m.season_id,
           bbb.bowler AS player_id,
           COUNT(*)   AS wickets,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY m.season_id
               ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, bbb.bowler
           ) AS rn
    FROM match AS m
    JOIN ball_by_ball ON ball_by_ball.match_id = m.match_id
    JOIN wicket_taken wt ON wt.match_id  = ball_by_ball.match_id
                        AND wt.over_id   = ball_by_ball.over_id
                        AND wt.ball_id   = ball_by_ball.ball_id
                        AND wt.innings_no= ball_by_ball.innings_no
    WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')
    GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.bowler
)
WHERE rn <= 3
ORDER BY season_id, rn
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Combine the two ranked lists position‑for‑position for the final season‑wide output
WITH top_batsmen AS (
    SELECT m.season_id,
           bbb.striker AS player_id,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS total_runs,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY m.season_id
               ORDER BY SUM(bs.runs_scored) DESC, bbb.striker
           ) AS pos
    FROM match AS m
    JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
    JOIN batsman_scored AS bs  ON bs.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                              AND bs.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                              AND bs.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                              AND bs.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
    GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.striker
),
top_bowlers AS (
    SELECT m.season_id,
           bbb.bowler  AS player_id,
           COUNT(*)    AS wickets,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
               PARTITION BY m.season_id
               ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, bbb.bowler
           ) AS pos
    FROM match AS m
    JOIN ball_by_ball   AS bbb ON bbb.match_id = m.match_id
    JOIN wicket_taken   AS wt  ON wt.match_id  = bbb.match_id
                              AND wt.over_id   = bbb.over_id
                              AND wt.ball_id   = bbb.ball_id
                              AND wt.innings_no= bbb.innings_no
    WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','hit wicket','retired hurt')
    GROUP BY m.season_id, bbb.bowler
)
SELECT tb.season_id,
       tb.player_id      AS batsman_id,
       tb.total_runs,
       bw.player_id      AS bowler_id,
       bw.wickets
FROM top_batsmen tb
JOIN top_bowlers bw
  ON bw.season_id = tb.season_id
 AND bw.pos       = tb.pos   -- match positions 1‑to‑1, 2‑to‑2, 3‑to‑3
WHERE tb.pos <= 3
ORDER BY tb.season_id, tb.pos
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query is restricted to a maximum of 20 rows and collectively they illustrate how to progress from basic inspections to the fully joined result set needed for the task.
